          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a dry cough for about ten days after a cold, no fever. Should I be doing anything?",
          "answer": "Based on the retrieved guidance, a dry cough that has lasted about ten days after a cold, without fever, falls within the expected pattern. The common cold guidance says cough often lasts two to three weeks, and that this is normal and not by itself a sign of a bacterial infection (*Upper respiratory infection (common cold)*). The antibiotic guidance says cough persisting two to three weeks after a cold is expected (*Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship*).\n\nThe safety check found no emergency pattern. That does not mean nothing is wrong. It only means the check did not match your description to its list. Get medical advice if you notice any of these:\n\n- The cough is getting worse, or you start to feel more short of breath.\n- You develop a fever, or you feel better for several days and then get worse. The influenza guidance describes this pattern as one worth recognizing, because it can signal a secondary bacterial pneumonia (*Influenza*).\n- A change in mucus color or amount, or coughing up blood.\n- The cough lasts well beyond three weeks, or you have a long-term lung or heart condition.\n\nThe retrieved material did not cover other causes of a persistent cough, such as asthma or reflux, so I can't say what else might be involved. A clinician can check your chest and your history. If you are unsure, book an appointment and describe the timeline as you have here.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
